##  Course Guideline: Paper Critique

As part of this course you critically assess a scientific paper and present that assessment to the class. Each of you picks a paper from the provided list on a first-come, first-served basis. The critique is delivered as a single **oral presentation**, worth **20% of your final mark**, and it is graded by me.

The task is meant to test whether you can read a paper closely, judge it on its merits, place it in the wider literature, and communicate all of that clearly to an audience. There is no written report to hand in; everything is assessed from the talk itself and the slides you present.

---

### Format

Your talk should run for **ten minutes**, followed by roughly **five minutes of questions** from me and the rest of the class. Presentations take place in **Week 10**. The order is set once everyone has chosen a paper, and I will circulate the schedule beforehand.

Prepare a small set of slides to support the talk. They are there to carry figures and key points, not to be read out word for word, so keep the text light and make sure everything on them is legible from the back of the room.

---

### What to cover

A good critique goes well beyond summarising the paper. Aim to spend most of your time on your own analysis. Concretely, I am looking for the following.

**Understanding.** Show that you have understood what the paper set out to do, how it went about it, and what it found. Be honest about the parts you found difficult, and explain why they were difficult, rather than glossing over them.

**Strengths and weaknesses.** This is the heart of the critique. Identify what the authors did well, such as a clear method or a robust result, and where the work is weaker, such as unstated assumptions, thin evidence, or overreaching conclusions. Justify each point.

**Scientific reception.** Look at how the community responded to the paper after publication. Give citation counts from a source such as Scopus, Google Scholar, or Web of Science, and describe how and why later work has cited it.

**Impact.** Comment on whether the paper actually changed anything in the field, and back your view with evidence rather than assertion. It is entirely acceptable to argue that a paper had little lasting impact, as long as you can support that.

**Presentation quality.** Deliver the talk clearly and confidently, keep to time, and use your slides effectively.

---

### Additional expectation for EMSC6025 (postgraduate)

Postgraduate students are expected to reach further into the literature. Choose **two subsequent papers that cited your chosen article** and, in your talk, explain why each citation was made, how the original paper influenced that later work, and whether the later authors captured its key contribution accurately. The mark allocation below is adjusted proportionally to make room for this.

---

## Tips for success

Focus on analysis rather than recap, and support your judgements with citations, examples, or data wherever you can. Keep a clear line between your own view and what the paper actually says. When you present, engage the audience with your voice and eye contact instead of reading from the slides, use visuals to highlight the points that matter, and stay within the time limit.

<<<

### Marking rubric

The talk is marked out of 20.

| Criterion | Marks |
|-----------|-------|
| Understanding of the paper | 4 |
| Strengths and weaknesses | 5 |
| Scientific reception | 3 |
| Impact on the field | 3 |
| Delivery (clarity, engagement, timing) | 2.5 |
| Slides (clarity, appropriateness, legibility) | 2.5 |

For EMSC6025 students the analysis of two citing papers is assessed as part of the "scientific reception" and "impact" criteria, with those weightings adjusted accordingly.

---

By the end of this assessment you should be able to critically evaluate scientific writing, communicate complex ideas clearly to an audience, and understand how an individual study fits into the broader arc of research in its field.
